<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Henry III, Margrave of Meissen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1215–1288</span> · Also known as Henry the Illustrious</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Henry III, Margrave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a>, called the Illustrious, was a Wettin prince who ruled Meissen from 1221. Through his mother, a Ludowingian, he claimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a> after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-raspe">Henry Raspe</a> and won it in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-thuringian-succession">War of the Thuringian Succession</a> by 1264. He was also a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minnesanger">Minnesinger</a> and patron of court culture, and his gains made the Wettins one of the leading houses of the empire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henry-iii-margrave-of-meissen.jpg" alt="Henry III, Margrave of Meissen" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Master of the Codex Manesse (Additional Painter I) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Codex_Manesse_Markgraf_Heinrich_von_Mei%C3%9Fen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
